/-!
# The scheme-theoretic support under a flat pullback

Let `f : Y ⟶ X` be a flat morphism of algebraic spaces over a scheme `S` and let `F` be a
quasi-coherent `𝒪_X`-module of finite type with scheme-theoretic support `Z ⊆ X`.  Stacks 089C
asserts that the inverse image `f⁻¹(Z) ⊆ Y` of Stacks 083Q is the scheme-theoretic support of
`f^*F`.

Both closed subspaces are cut out by an ideal sheaf which is computed over a diagram of affine
étale charts.  Over such a diagram, with chart `U` of `Y` above chart `V` of `X`, the defining
ideal of `f⁻¹(Z)` is the ideal generated by the image of the defining ideal of `Z`
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_preimage_eq_map_chartDiagram`), the defining ideal of
`Z` is the annihilator of the sections of `F` over `V`
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartIdeal_annihilatorIdealSheaf_eq_annihilator`), and the annihilator
ideal of `f^*F` is the annihilator of its sections over `U`.  Flatness of `f` makes the
restriction map `𝒪_X(V) → 𝒪_Y(U)` flat, the sections of `F` over `V` are a finite module, and
the sections of `f^*F` over `U` are their base change; so the two annihilators are related by
the flat base change of Stacks 07T8,
`ann_extension_eq_ann_baseChange_module`.  That identity on affine chart diagrams is local on
`Y`, and both ideal sheaves are sheaves, so it propagates to the equality of ideal sheaves and
hence of closed subspaces.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.flat_chartDiagramRestrict_of_isFlat`: for a flat morphism the
  restriction map of regular functions along a diagram of affine étale charts is flat.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.annihilatorIdealAt_modulePullback_eq_map`: over a diagram of affine
  étale charts the annihilator ideal of `f^*F` is generated by the image of the annihilator
  ideal of `F`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealSheaf_preimage_eq_annihilatorIdealSheaf`: the defining
  ideal sheaf of `f⁻¹(Z)` is the annihilator ideal sheaf of `f^*F`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.schemeTheoreticSupport_modulePullback`: **Stacks 089C.**

## Implementation notes

**Nothing is carried.**  The affine-chart description of `f^*F`, Descent, Proposition 03DX(2)
read across `f`, enters through
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCartesianOnAffineChartDiagrams_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite`, which
derives it from quasi-coherence of `F` alone;
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCartesianOnAffineCharts_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite` does not supply it,
comparing as it does two charts of a single algebraic space.  So the only hypotheses below are
flatness of the morphism, quasi-coherence of `F` and finiteness of its type, which are the
hypotheses of the tag.

**The covering of charts and the quasi-coherence of the pullback are proved, not carried.**  The
affine étale charts of `Y` which are the source of an affine chart diagram over `f` cover every
chart of `Y`: an affine chart of `Y` need not map into a single affine chart of `X`, so this is a
statement about refining charts, and it is the cofinality of Stacks 03G2,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.generate_affineChartDiagram_mem_topology`, whose condition on the chart
of `X` is instantiated at finiteness of the sections of `F` there.  Quasi-coherence of `f^*F` on
the small étale site of `Y` is Stacks 03GA, which
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_modulePullback_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite` proves
for an arbitrary morphism from that same cofinality.

**Flatness is not carried.**  It is the hypothesis of the tag, and it is consumed here and
nowhere else: `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsFlat` is flatness of the scheme morphism underlying
every commuting square of étale charts, which is exactly the vertical morphism of a chart
diagram, so the flat base change of annihilators applies to the chart rings directly.

The scheme-theoretic support is `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.schemeTheoreticSupport`, the
intersection of the closed subspaces whose defining ideal annihilates the module, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_isClosedSubspace_schemeTheoreticSupport` identifies it with the
closed subspace cut out by the annihilator ideal sheaf; the statement below is therefore an
equality of subfunctors, read through
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.schemeTheoreticSupport_eq_of_definingIdealSheaf_eq`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Lemma 67.30.10 (Tag 089C)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/089C)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07U0](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07U0)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07U1](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07U1)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07T8](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07T8)
